{"context":"The EKA Standard corpus declared 1.3 frozen at commit d509e9c (the shr- sharing-object contract), but three additive normative commits landed on that snapshot afterwards without a bump: f90b59c (scope-aware container concurrency — specification/concepts.md, state.md), c185835 (validity horizon — specification/exchange.md, overview.md, rules/rule-001.md), 30269b3 (provenance and capture envelope — specification/exchange.md), plus 06944e4 (the exchange conformance corpus under conformance/exchange/). versioning.md's 1.1 bullet already defines a minor version as backward-compatible normative change and states 1.3 is never edited in place, so the corpus contradicted its own rule. Tracked as eka/bug:standard-version-bump-conflict:1. No git tag carries the version either way: eka-standard carries v1.0, v1.1, v1.2 only.","decision":"Cut EKA Standard 1.4 as an additive minor and classify the three post-1.3 normative commits plus the conformance corpus into it. 1.3 remains exactly the shr- contract (d509e9c) and is tagged v1.3; 1.4 is tagged at the head that carries the sync set. Freezing is a property of the declaration, not of the git tag: once versioning.md names a version as the frozen snapshot, any further normative edit — including documenting an already-implemented optional field — belongs to the next number. The full sync set is applied in one change: specification/versioning.md (1.4 snapshot line, 1.4 bullet naming the three commits, version axes table), CHANGELOG.md (new 1.4 section), EKA (Version line to 1.4 and the index topic for specification/exchange.md extended to name the envelope fields), specification/overview.md, specification/exchange.md (corpus version), README.md (version table and the 1.3 prose), eka-core/exchange/model.go (SpecificationVersion to 1.4 — locked to the EKA Version line by standardembed_test.go and cmd/version_test.go), eka-core/exchange/deserialize.go (the accepted specification-version set widened to 1.4, 1.3, 1.1, 1.0 so 1.3 packages stay readable), the vendored eka-cli/EKA copy and its vendored.yaml pin (standardRelease 1.4 with the refreshed sha256). Tagging v1.3 and v1.4 stays a human action; the verification scripts never bump, tag or push.","consequences":"Positive: the append-only claim stays verifiable, the corpus and the exchange specification version cannot drift (a build-time test locks them), and the pre-tag gate (scripts/verify-release.sh, seven axes) catches a missed axis on the next bump. Negative: every exported package now declares specificationVersion 1.4, so a 1.3 consumer must re-import to adopt it, and the importer must be widened or a 1.3 package would be refused; the bump carries no behavioural change beyond the declared conformance version, so it is bookkeeping rather than capability. Neutral: M1 (the appliesTo knowledge-to-scope binding and eka context --for) inherits 1.5 rather than 1.4.","alternativesConsidered":"1) Option (a), a clarification carve-out on the 1.1 bullet so documenting an already-implemented optional field needs no bump — rejected: it makes the corpus's append-only claim unverifiable and lets a consumer's 1.3 diverge from the published bytes, which is the bug's own complaint. 2) Fold the three additive commits into the still-untagged 1.3 and reserve 1.4 for M1 — the cheapest coherent path (no version-number change, no importer change, no code cascade) — rejected because versioning.md had already declared 1.3 frozen and the standing precedent (eka/adr:sharing-object-model:1 records that a new standard token means a standard change) treats a normative corpus change as a version change; folding also leaves the freeze claim dependent on tag timing. 3) Cut 1.4 for the backlog but hold it open until M1 lands so M1 ships as 1.4 — rejected: a declared version that keeps accreting is the same in-place-edit problem in a new place. Chosen: cut 1.4 for the backlog now; M1 is 1.5.","status":"proposed"}